djb_false_proofs:
I think this is misleading for the proof-checkers in systems where multiple provers must pass for the proof to be accepted.
In the case of Metamath, yes, soundness bugs have been found in the original &quot;metamath&quot; proof verifier, written in C. But to be accepted into the main proof databases (set.mm and iset.mm), proofs must pass &quot;5 independently-created verifiers in 5 different programming languages: metamath (a C verifier by Norm Megill), mmj2 (a Java verifier by Mel O'Cat and Mario Carneiro), smetamath-rs (a high-speed Rust verifier by Stefan O'Rear), checkmm (a C++ verifier by Eric Schmidt), and mmverify.py a Python verifier by Raph Levien).&quot; - https:&#x2F;&#x2F;us.metamath.org&#x2F;
To me, that's a key countermeasure to the problem.
